12:45:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’ve noticed a real inconsistency, and you’re right to question it. The reason older movies often keep their music on TV is because **the way music rights were sold has changed dramatically over time**. ### 1. In the “Old Days,” Rights Were Often Sold Forever (or at Least Very Broadly) Before the 1980s or 1990s, music publishers and record labels commonly sold **“all media” or “perpetual” licenses** to film studios. This meant the studio could use the song in the movie **and** in any future distribution format that existed or might ever exist—TV, home video, etc. The deal was: > *“You pay us once, you can use this song in your movie forever, in any way you want.”* This was standard practice because: - There were far fewer distribution channels (theatrical, TV, maybe VHS eventually). - No one imagined streaming or digital downloads. - The music industry was still figuring out how to maximize revenue from film placements. So yes, for many movies from the 1950s through the 1980s, the original music **is locked in forever** (or at least for a very long time). That’s why you can watch *The Breakfast Club* or *Ghostbusters* on TV today and hear the original songs—the contracts were all‑inclusive. ### 2. The Shift: “Format‑Specific” Licenses Became the Norm Starting in the late 1980s and accelerating in the 1990s, music rights holders realized they were leaving money on the table. They began insisting on **separate licenses for each format**: theatrical, VHS, DVD, broadcast TV, cable, streaming, etc. Each new format required a new negotiation (and a new payment). This became standard practice because: - Home video (VHS, DVD) exploded and generated huge revenue. - Streaming emerged later as a completely new medium not covered in older contracts. - Rights holders wanted to capture that value. So for movies made after roughly 1990, the music license is often **limited** to what was originally bargained for—usually just theatrical and sometimes one home video release. Everything else (streaming, digital downloads, some TV airings) requires a separate fee. ### 3. TV Broadcasts: Often Still Covered by Older Deals Even for newer films, TV broadcast rights are sometimes included in the original theatrical license (or can be negotiated as a package). But streaming is rarely included automatically because it didn’t exist when the contract was written. That’s why you might see the same movie on cable TV with the original music, but on Netflix with a substitute. ### To Summarize: - **Older movies (pre‑1990s)** – music rights were typically sold perpetually and broadly, so the music never changes. - **Newer movies (1990s onward)** – rights are format‑specific and time‑limited, so music may be swapped for streaming, digital, or re‑releases. - **TV broadcasts** – can be included in the original deal or re‑negotiated separately, but often they are covered (especially for older films) because TV was an existing format when the contract was signed. So your observation is correct: *some* movies do have “forever” music rights. But the industry changed, and now most movies don’t. That’s why you see inconsistent results—some films keep their songs forever, while others swap them out for new releases.
